00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.200
hello and welcome I'm James Murphy and today&nbsp;
we're taking a look at a subtle rule that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.720
python has about variable scopes. let's&nbsp;
play a game. what does the function print?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.240
let's start with an easy one, shout out the&nbsp;
answers as we go. tell me either what it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.840
going to print or what error it's going to give.&nbsp;
let's start with f what do you think happens when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.240
we run it? well of course when we run it we&nbsp;
get an error, specifically a name error. okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.240
the first one was really easy what about the&nbsp;
second one, g. is it any different from f?

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:42.000
well of course we still get an error, but it's&nbsp;
actually a different error. now, technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.960
unbound local error derives from name error, but&nbsp;
I think it might be surprising to many of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:54.000
that something different happened at all. somehow&nbsp;
python knew that I was going to assign to x later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.320
in the function. normally in python, outside of a&nbsp;
looping context, we don't expect statements in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.480
function that are later in the function to affect&nbsp;
statements that are earlier in the function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.560
it turns out that whether or not a&nbsp;
variable like x is determined to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.800
free variable or a local variable is actually&nbsp;
determined at compile time, not at runtime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.960
in the function f, there are no assignments&nbsp;
to the variable x so x is determined to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.280
free variable. that means when we say print x it&nbsp;
tries to look up x in the global scope and fails.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.680
in g, however, the function does contain an&nbsp;
assignment for x, it says x equals 1. so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.920
means that in the function g, x is determined to&nbsp;
be a local variable. that means that when we try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.760
to print x it looks it up in the local scope and&nbsp;
hence we get a local error, not a global error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.080
keeping that in mind, let's look at another&nbsp;
two examples and see if you get them right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.720
what do you think happens when we call h? we&nbsp;
have defined the global variable y equals 0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:05.280
before defining the function and then we try to&nbsp;
print y in the function. go on just shout it out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.320
okay this was another easy one, it prints zero.&nbsp;
now what do you think happens for the j function?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.840
take your time, I'll wait. okay have&nbsp;
you locked your guess in? here we go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:30.080
when I run the j function, I get another unbound&nbsp;
local error. now you might look at this and say:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.400
when I call j, we have this global variable y&nbsp;
that's defined to be zero, so print y should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.600
printing 0 just like in the previous case. but&nbsp;
that's not how the variable lookup rules work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.800
because somewhere in the function j assigns y a&nbsp;
value, everywhere in the function y is treated as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.000
a local variable. that means at the beginning when&nbsp;
we try to print y, it doesn't even try to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.760
it up in the global scope. it only looks in the&nbsp;
local scope. this can be extra confusing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.480
this assignment y equals 1 could theoretically&nbsp;
be hundreds of lines later in the function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.040
this is yet another reason to avoid&nbsp;
using global variables if you can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.120
I also want to point out that other languages&nbsp;
like c and c++ do not do things this way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.480
when I run this function it first prints&nbsp;
the global I and then prints the local I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:28.320
that's why we get 0 and then 1 down at the bottom&nbsp;
here. I even compile with -Wall and -Wextra to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.760
give me extra warnings and still no warnings to&nbsp;
be seen. if I want to get a warning for this kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.680
of behavior I have to compile with -Wshadow. the&nbsp;
shadow here is referring to the fact that what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.520
doing is called variable shadowing. if I have a&nbsp;
variable in an inner scope like inside a function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.400
where I define a variable that has the same&nbsp;
name as another variable in an outer scope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.600
like the global scope, then this is called&nbsp;
"variable shadowing". in this case I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.120
say that this variable i shadows the one&nbsp;
in the global scope. for obvious reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.160
you should really try to avoid this if at all&nbsp;
possible. of course the same goes for python,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.000
shadowing variables puts you on the fast track&nbsp;
to writing bugs. in case you were ever wondering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.280
this is actually one of the reasons why I define&nbsp;
a separate main function rather than just putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.880
things down here. any assignments that I do&nbsp;
down here are actually in the global scope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.400
and that means there's a chance that they're being&nbsp;
shadowed. I might expect an error if I try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.240
print y here but because the y down here is in the&nbsp;
global scope I actually just print that y instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.600
on the other hand if I define a main function&nbsp;
then any assignments in here that I do are local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.960
to that function. that means anything that I do&nbsp;
in here I don't have to worry about affecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:51.200
things in other functions like h. now when I&nbsp;
run the function I get the expected error in h.

